  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nSurface high pressure is drifting off the east coast. Meanwhile, a\nclosed mid-level high is setting up over the southeastern states.\nThis will result in a pattern change and deep-layer theta-e advection\ncoming into the Ohio Valley starting today. This change in air mass\nwill result in highs in the mid 80s to lower 90s, and dewpoints\nreaching the upper 60s to around 70. Not out of the question that\nhead indices could briefly approach 100 in the far southwestern ILN \nCWA.\n\nWhile an initial shortwave today will remain well north of the \nregion, a frontal zone will set up over the Great Lakes, eventually \nleading to increasing precipitation chances across the ILN forecast \narea. For tonight into Wednesday morning, the convective forecast for\nthe ILN CWA is not particularly high confidence. Storms are expected\nto initiate over Illinois and northern Indiana during the evening,\nmoving east toward the northwestern corner of the ILN CWA in the late\nevening through the overnight hours. The SPC Day 1 MRGL risk area\nindicates the area where this may occur. It is not entirely sure if\nthe storms will hold together -- some CAMs are quite spotty with\ncoverage. If they do, while parameters are not overly impressive,\nthere appears to be just enough CAPE and shear to support a marginal\nsevere threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nMost of the rest of the week will be characterized by high theta-e\nvalues (with heat and humidity) on the northern periphery of the\nridge, with convective chances initiating from the frontal zone on\nthe northern edge of the warm sector. Timing on convective chances\nremains somewhat uncertain, as antecedent convection may play a role,\nas will fast-moving shortwaves moving through the westerly upper\nflow.\n\nAlthough convection could disrupt the temperature forecast, as of now\nit appears both Wednesday and Thursday will feature highs in the mid\n80s to lower 90s. With dewpoints getting into the lower 70s, some\nareas in the southern half of the forecast area may approach heat\nadvisory criteria (100 degree heat indices).\n\nAs of now, it appears heavy rain will be the main convective threat.\nShear will be marginal, though a strong storm or two could not be\ntotally discounted given the expected instability (SBCAPE of\n1000-2000 J/kg). Rather, it will be the potential for repeated rounds\nof storms that create greater concern for flooding -- especially in\nan environment with precipitable water values of around 2 inches.\n\nMight have some additional storm chances on Friday and into the\nweekend, but confidence in the forecast decreases considerably once\nthe ridge begins to break down. Slightly cooler temperatures look \nlikely for Friday, Saturday, and Sunday, compared to the middle of \nthe week.",
